ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Sieber JR, McInerney MJ, Gunsalus RP. 2012. Genomic insights into syntrophy: the paradigm for anaerobic metabolic cooperation. Annu. Rev. Microbiol. 66:429&#x2013;452. 10.1146/annurev-micro-090110-102844.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1146/annurev-micro-090110-102844</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">22803797</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Schink B. 1997. Energetics of syntrophic cooperation in methanogenic degradation. Microbiol. Mol. Biol. Rev. 61:262&#x2013;280.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC232610</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">9184013</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Plugge CM, Zhang W, Scholten JC, Stams AJ. 2011. Metabolic flexibility of sulfate-reducing bacteria. Front. Microbiol. 2:81. 10.3389/fmicb.2011.00081.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3389/fmicb.2011.00081</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC3119409</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21734907</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Stams AJM, Plugge CM. 2009. Electron transfer in syntrophic communities of anaerobic bacteria and archaea. Nat. Rev. Microbiol. 7:568&#x2013;577. 10.1038/nrmicro2166.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/nrmicro2166</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">19609258</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Karakashev D, Batstone DJ, Trably E, Angelidaki I. 2006. Acetate oxidation is the dominant methanogenic pathway from acetate in the absence of Methanosaetaceae. Appl. Environ. Microbiol. 72:5138&#x2013;5141. 10.1128/AEM.00489-06.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1128/AEM.00489-06</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC1489330</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16820524</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Peura S, Eiler A, Bertilsson S, Nykanen H, Tiirola M, Jones RI. 2012. Distinct and diverse anaerobic bacterial communities in boreal lakes dominated by candidate division OD1. ISME J. 6:1640&#x2013;1652. 10.1038/ismej.2012.21.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/ismej.2012.21</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC3498924</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">22418623</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">25141130</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2015</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>09</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2021</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>21</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic-eCollection"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1932-6203</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>9</Volume><Issue>8</Issue><PubDate><Year>2014</Year></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>PloS one</Title><ISOAbbreviation>PLoS One</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>A long-term cultivation of an anaerobic methane-oxidizing microbial community from deep-sea methane-seep sediment using a continuous-flow bioreactor.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>e105356</StartPage><MedlinePgn>e105356</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">e105356</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1371/journal.pone.0105356</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Anaerobic oxidation of methane (AOM) in marine sediments is an important global methane sink, but the physiological characteristics of AOM-associated microorganisms remain poorly understood. Here we report the cultivation of an AOM microbial community from deep-sea methane-seep sediment using a continuous-flow bioreactor with polyurethane sponges, called the down-flow hanging sponge (DHS) bioreactor. We anaerobically incubated deep-sea methane-seep sediment collected from the Nankai Trough, Japan, for 2,013 days in the bioreactor at 10&#xb0;C. Following incubation, an active AOM activity was confirmed by a tracer experiment using 13C-labeled methane. Phylogenetic analyses demonstrated that phylogenetically diverse Archaea and Bacteria grew in the bioreactor. After 2,013 days of incubation, the predominant archaeal components were anaerobic methanotroph (ANME)-2a, Deep-Sea Archaeal Group, and Marine Benthic Group-D, and Gammaproteobacteria was the dominant bacterial lineage. Fluorescence in situ hybridization analysis showed that ANME-1 and -2a, and most ANME-2c cells occurred without close physical interaction with potential bacterial partners. Our data demonstrate that the DHS bioreactor system is a useful system for cultivating fastidious methane-seep-associated sedimentary microorganisms.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Aoki</LastName><ForeName>Masataka</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Subsurface Geobiological Analysis and Research (D-SUGAR), Japan Agency for Marine-Earth Science and Technology (JAMSTEC), Yokosuka, Kanagawa, Japan; Department of Environmental Systems Engineering, Nagaoka University of Technology, Nagaoka, Niigata, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ehara</LastName><ForeName>Masayuki</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Environmental Systems Engineering, Nagaoka University of Technology, Nagaoka, Niigata, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Saito</LastName><ForeName>Yumi</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Subsurface Geobiological Analysis and Research (D-SUGAR), Japan Agency for Marine-Earth Science and Technology (JAMSTEC), Yokosuka, Kanagawa, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yoshioka</LastName><ForeName>Hideyoshi</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Institute for Geo-resources and Environment, National Institute of Advanced Industrial Science and Technology (AIST), Tsukuba, Ibaraki, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Miyazaki</LastName><ForeName>Masayuki</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Subsurface Geobiological Analysis and Research (D-SUGAR), Japan Agency for Marine-Earth Science and Technology (JAMSTEC), Yokosuka, Kanagawa, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Saito</LastName><ForeName>Yayoi</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Subsurface Geobiological Analysis and Research (D-SUGAR), Japan Agency for Marine-Earth Science and Technology (JAMSTEC), Yokosuka, Kanagawa, Japan; Department of Environmental Systems Engineering, Nagaoka University of Technology, Nagaoka, Niigata, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Miyashita</LastName><ForeName>Ai</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Environmental Systems Engineering, Nagaoka University of Technology, Nagaoka, Niigata, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kawakami</LastName><ForeName>Shuji</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Environmental Systems Engineering, Nagaoka University of Technology, Nagaoka, Niigata, Japan; Department of Construction Systems Engineering, Anan National College of Technology, Anan, Tokushima, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yamaguchi</LastName><ForeName>Takashi</ForeName><Initials>T</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Environmental Systems Engineering, Nagaoka University of Technology, Nagaoka, Niigata, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ohashi</LastName><ForeName>Akiyoshi</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Social and Environmental Engineering, Hiroshima University