igestibility measurements in grey kangaroo and sheep. Austral J Biol Sci. 1976;29:209&#x2013;214.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">985222</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Klieve AV, Hudman JF, Bauchop T. Inducible bacteriophages from ruminal bacteria. Appl Environ Microbiol. 1989;55:1630&#x2013;1634.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC202916</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">2504111</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Klieve AV, Ouwerkerk D, Maguire AJ. Archaea in the foregut of macropod marsupials: PCR and amplicon sequence-based observations. J Appl Microbiol. 2012;113:1065&#x2013;1075.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">22897363</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Krzycki JA, Kenealy WR, DeNiro MJ, Zeikus JG. Stable carbon isotope fractionation by Methanosarcina barkeri during methanogenesis from acetate, methanol or carbon dioxide-hydrogen. Appl Environ Microbiol. 1987;53:2597&#x2013;2599.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC204153</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16347476</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lassey KR. Livestock methane emission: from the individual grazing animal through national inventories to the global methane cycle. Agri Forest Meteorol. 2007;142:120&#x2013;132.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Liu C, Finegold SM, Song Y, Lawson PA. Reclassification of Clostridium coccoides, Ruminococcus hansenii, Ruminococcus hydrogenotrophicus, Ruminococcus luti, Ruminococcus productus and Ruminococcus schinkii as Blautia coccoides gen. nov., comb. nov., Blautia hansenii comb. nov., Blautia hydrogenotrophica comb. nov., Blautia luti comb. nov., Blautia producta comb. nov., Blautia schinkii comb. nov. and description of Blautia wexlerae sp. nov., isolated from human faeces. Int J Syst Evol Microbiol. 2008;58:1896&#x2013;1902.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">18676476</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Neufeld JD, Sch&#xe4;fer H, Cox MJ, Boden R, McDonald IR, Murrell JC. Stable-isotope probing implicates Methylophaga spp and novel Gammaproteobacteria in marine methanol and methylamine metabolism. ISME J. 2007;1:480&#x2013;491.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">18043650</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ouwerkerk D, Klieve AV, Forster RJ, Templeton JM, Maguire AJ. Characterization of culturable anaerobic bacteria from the forestomach of an eastern grey kangaroo, Macropus giganteus. Lett Appl Microbiol. 2005;41:327&#x2013;333.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16162139</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ouwerkerk D, Maguire AJ, McMillen L, Klieve AV. Hydrogen utilising bacteria from the forestomach of eastern grey (Macropus giganteus) and red (Macropus rufus) kangaroos. Animal Production Sci. 2009;49:1043&#x2013;1051.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Penning H, Claus P, Casper P, Conrad R. Carbon isotope fractionation during acetoclastic methanogenesis by Methanosaeta concilii in culture and a lake sediment. Appl Environ Microbiol. 2006;72:5648&#x2013;5652.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC1538750</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16885323</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Pennington R. The metabolism of short-chain fatty acids in the sheep. 1. Fatty acid utilization and ketone body production by rumen epithelium and other tissues. Biochem J. 1952;51:251.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC1197830</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">14944582</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Pope PB, Denman SE, Jones M, Tringe SG, Barry K, Malfatti SA, et al. Adaptation to herbivory by the Tammar wallaby includes bacterial and glycoside hydrolase profiles different from other herbivores. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA. 2010;107:14793&#x2013;14798.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC2930436</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">20668243</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Sheppach W. Effects of short chain fatty acids on gut morphology and function. Gut. 1994;35:S35&#x2013;S38.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC1378144</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">8125387</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Sokal RR, Rohlf FJ. Biometry. WH Freeman and Company: San Francisco, CA, USA; 1969.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Vreman HJ, Dowling JA, Raubach RA, Weiner MW. Determination of acetate in biological material by vacuum microdistillation and gas chromatography. Anal Chem. 1978;50:1138&#x2013;1141.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">677464</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Whiteley AS, Thomson B, Lueders T, Manefield M. RNA stable-isotope probing. Nat Protoc. 2007;2:838&#x2013;844.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">17446884</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wolin MJ, Miller TL, Stewart CS.1997Microbe-microbe interactionsIn: Hobson PN, Stewart CS, (eds)The Rumen Microbial System Chapman and Hall: London, UK; 467&#x2013;488.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Yu Z, Forster RJ.2005Nucelic acid extraction, oligonucleotide probes and PCR methodsIn: Makkar HPS, McSweeny CS, (eds)Methods in Gut Microbial Ecology for Ruminants Springer: Dordrecht, Netherlands; 81&#x2013;104.</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Curated"><PMID Version="1">24606006</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2014</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>01</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2018</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>02</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1365-2672</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>117</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2014</Year><Month>Jul</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Journal of applied microbiology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>J Appl Microbiol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Pyrosequencing reveals correlations between extremely acidophilic bacterial communities with hydrogen sulphide concentrations, pH and inert polymer coatings at concrete sewer crown surfaces.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>50</StartPage><EndPage>64</EndPage><MedlinePgn>50-64</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/jam.12491</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the acidophilic bacterial communities involved in microbially induced concrete corrosion (MICC).</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS AND RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Our study sites located downstream from a forced main provided a unique opportunity to study the microbial communities involved in MICC under different environmental conditions (gradients of atmospheric H(2)S, sulphate concentration and pH) and under pipe modifications (coated vs uncoated). Bacterial cell density estimated by both cultivation- and DNA-based methods was low in the corroded sewer samples. Pyrosequencing and cloning showed that Mycobacterium and Acidithiobacillus dominated the acidophilic microbial communities. Methylacidiphilum was also dominant in samples where methane was detected. Correlation analysis indicated that Mycobacterium and Acidithiobacillus were significantly affected by pH and that Mycobacterium could better withstand highly acidic conditions compared to Acidithiobacillus.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Communities dominated by Mycobacterium favoured conditions in the lined sewer pipes, while communities with a higher relative abundance of Acidithiobacillus favoured the unlined sewer pipes.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="SIGNIFICANCE AND IMPACT OF THE STUDY" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Identifying the key micro-organisms involved in MICC and knowing how they interact with their environment are essential aspects for identifying steps towards concrete corrosion management.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2014 The Society for Applied Microbiology.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Pagaling</LastName><ForeName>E</ForeName><Initials>E</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of Hawai'i at M&#x101;noa, Honolulu, HI, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yang</LastName><ForeName>K</ForeName><Initials>K</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yan</LastName><ForeName>T</ForeName><Initials>T</Initials></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D013485">Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2014</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>28</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>England</Country><MedlineTA>J Appl Microbiol</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>9706280</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>1364-5072</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><ChemicalList><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D012336">RNA, Ribosomal, 16S</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D012722">Sewage</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>YY9FVM7NSN</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D006862">Hydrogen Sulfide</NameOfSubstance></Chemical></ChemicalList><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D042763" MajorTopicYN="N">Acidithiobacillus</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000145" MajorTopicYN="N">classification</QualifierName><QualifierName UI="Q000235" MajorTopicYN="Y"